[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609091200-a-definition-is-the-unit-of-a-library"
 :adr/title "A definition is the unit of a library: kotoba.string as 59 repos plus a facade"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-09"
 :adr/authority "owner directive, 2026-09-09"
 :adr/body "Owner directive, 2026-09-09: stop at clojure.string and use kotoba.string --
and do not bundle it into one lib or one repo. Split it, sub-imports included,
one repo at a time in the kotoba-lang org, with libraries re-importing each
other. IPLD- and Unison-shaped library management.

## What changes

The unit of a library becomes the DEFINITION, not the namespace.

kotoba.lang.text was one file, 828 lines, 59 definitions, one repo. It becomes
59 repos -- one per definition -- plus a facade, kotoba.string, that holds no
implementation and re-exports them. Each definition repo names, in its own
deps.edn, exactly the definitions its body reaches. Nothing else.

The name follows clojure.string rather than the layer it used to sit in. That
also separates two things that were sharing a prefix: kotoba.lang.* keeps the
LANGUAGE (capability-*, conformance.*, code-identity), and the standard library
moves out to kotoba.* -- kotoba.string, and on the same argument kotoba.coll,
kotoba.edn, kotoba.fs, kotoba.io, kotoba.http, kotoba.process, kotoba.spec.
That is the same cut Clojure makes between clojure.core and clojure.string.

## The dependency graph is measured, not designed

The split follows the edges the code already has. 59 definitions, 55 edges, 26
leaves, acyclic, topologically ordered 59/59. Examples:

  split          -> match-spans
  split-lines    -> split
  trim / triml   -> java-whitespace?
  capitalize     -> lower, upper
  rewrite        -> match-spans, replacement-for
  replace        -> rewrite
  format         -> fmt-one, re-find, spec-re

match-spans is the single definition that touches the host regex engine, and
after the split that is visible in the DAG rather than in a comment: every
regex-shaped function reaches it and nothing else does.

The graph was extracted twice before it was believed. The first attempt matched
identifiers without skipping strings and comments and reported `split` -- which
is implemented on top of match-spans -- as a leaf, because every docstring in
that file names the functions around it. The second checked only a token's
FIRST occurrence, so `trim` came out a leaf too: the first `java-whitespace?`
in its form is inside prose. Both were caught by reading the implementations
that the graph claimed had no edges.

## What was verified before any repo was created

The existing 33-input parity corpus, which compares this implementation against
clojure.string as an ORACLE, was pointed at the split kotoba.string: 6,530
assertions, 0 failures. The one error is a test that opens
src/kotoba/lang/text.cljc by path to assert the namespace no longer requires
clojure.string -- an assertion about file layout, not about an answer, and that
file does not exist in this layout.

So the split changes no answer. That was established with a local :local/root
tree, before a single GitHub repo existed.

## Consequences, stated rather than discovered later

- A private definition stops being private. defn- and ^:private both had to go:
  another repo has to be able to require what it depends on. 14 of the 59 were
  private.
- A facade that re-exports must bind vars, not :refer them. A first version
  used :refer, which makes the names usable inside kotoba.string and leaves
  kotoba.string/split unresolvable from outside -- the one thing a facade is
  for.
- Every definition that shadows clojure.core needs its own :refer-clojure
  :exclude. The list is taken from the namespace being split rather than
  guessed, so the pieces cannot disagree with the whole they came from.
- Dependencies are pinned by content, in topological order: a repo is pushed
  only after everything it reaches has been pushed, and it names those at the
  sha they were pushed at. That is the IPLD half of the directive.
- kotoba.lang.text stays where it is for now. Nothing is deleted until the call
  sites move, and the call sites are ~11,900 files.

## What this does NOT decide

Whether the other stdlib families split the same way. The argument is the same
and the directive says all of them, but each family gets its own measured graph
before its repos exist -- this one produced two wrong graphs before a right
one, and that was with 59 definitions in a file I had just written."
